{
  "id": "COMPOUND:26277 - PROTEIN:5793",
  "verbalization": "Stecholide I is implicated in the biological activity of Hepatocyte through a series of molecular interactions. The evidence suggests that Stecholide I acts as a target for CMP-N-acetylneuraminate-beta-1,4-galactoside alpha-2,3-sialyltransferase, which is in turn targeted by multiple compounds, including Leosibirone B and 6'-O-Z-Para-Methoxycinnamoylharpagide. These compounds are all linked to Hepatocyte as their targets. The integration of these pathways implies that Stecholide I may exert its effects by modulating the activity of these enzymes, potentially influencing cellular processes in the liver. The shared target of these compounds with Hepatocyte suggests a possible mechanism of action involving the regulation of sialyltransferase activity, which is critical for cellular functions such as signal transduction and cell surface recognition. The evidence supports a role for Stecholide I in the context of Hepatocyte regulation through this enzymatic pathway, though the exact mechanism of interaction remains to be fully elucidated.",
